Z||000000||FieroSTETZ||10-26-2001||06:06 AM||||wondering if someone can help me out with this- i am a brainless 19 year old w/ very little money, trying to discern if its worth me spending my money on. <P>heres the story- I have worked as a technician for almost 2 years at a compusa store in california. during the entire time of my employment there, i have been a highly regarded employee, constantly being accredited for excellent customer service, excellent productivity, and great knowledge of my job. My current position is Lead Technician, my job is to train new techs, and insure that everyone has me available for help, as well as being the major moneymaker. about 6 months ago, a fellow employee was accused of theft, suspended, and summarily fored- normal, right? no- the manager who fired him has been stealing the whole time he has worked here, and framed the employee during an audit. since then, i have been collecting evidence of theft (returned product that was never purchased, etc...) and everytime this stuff gets noticed, we lose a cashier. I took a big stack of such receipts and other proof of wrongdoing, and faxed them to a contact at corporate HR, the " good guys" not even an hour later, i am called into the GM's office and yelled at for going over his head about this, i don't back down, and let him know that i know he is protecting someone who is stealing merchandise, yada yada yada...<P>recently, as i have been faxing in MORE proof, this manager decides to try and lose me as an employee and begins paying extremely close attention to everything i do. thats not so bad, i haven't done anything wrong, i do my work like i am supposed to- he was not able to find anything that can justify termination of my employment, so he then begins telling other employees to try and get dirt on me, because i am a thief and have been stealing product since i started working here 2 years ago (not true) this pisses me off, attacking my integrity and such. as of yet, he still has been unable to prove anything, but i know its only a matter of time before they manufacture something. I am trying to find some way to take legal action in the event i do get fired on trumped up accusations. <P>questions-<BR>flat out called me a thief behind my back, does this constitute slander?<P>one of the managers followed a fellow employee and i out during our lunch break, and became physically agitated and tried to get us to fight him- upon returning to the store we told the gm, and he did nothing. is this harassment or anything? i mean, we did fear that he would do something, this guy is a known alcoholic (violent one)<P>what else can i do to build a fairly stron case against them? I don't want money out of this, i just want to insure that i cause the company enough trouble that they lose the two managers that are ripping off the store and treating the employees badly. i have testifiable witnesses for EVERYTHING stated above, and need to find out if i have enough to warrant going to a lawyer and spending the money to get something done. anyone who answers, gives advice, etc.. - i owe you big! let me know what i can do! i am currently selling my fiero as i am going to need the money, and cannot buy a newer car like i was planning, if my job is in danger. <BR>thank you so much, and if anyone wants to reach me off board, email me at fierostetz@home.com post questions if you need more details. sorry if rambling, just finished working 10AM thursday to 2:30 AM friday. thanks so so much!||||reg||1
Z||000001||rogergarrison||10-26-2001||06:52 AM||||If it was me, Id keep going to higher ups untill someone really does pay attention to you. Any one that gives you crap is probably doing dishonest things themselves. Sooner or later your effort will be rewarded. Company executives will always be happy to find out what your saying is going on, its costing them money. BUT be aware, your going to take the wraith of those you accuse and could be fired by them if their over you. You can still take it to the attention of higher ups who would possibly rehire you, maybe even to a higher up position yourself. You could get the reputation of being a 'rat' too. As far as legally going to court for slander, I dont think the effort would be worth it. You usually have to prove that what they said had a detremental effect on you (ie loss of employment, your standing in community)Another possibility is to turn in your  detailed resignation and go to another company so its in writing why you quit. If you do that, send the letter registered mail so you have a reciept of sending it from whoever gets it so it dont 'disappear'. Make sure you keep a copy too.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Jake_Dragon||10-26-2001||07:35 AM||||Wrongful termination.<BR>The employees that were let go should be told and let them make some calls. ||||reg||13
Z||000003||grinthock||10-26-2001||07:42 AM||||Been through a similar situation.  I was put on suspension while they investigated.  I decided to leave, had other job offers anyway.  I AM NOT suggesting you do the same thing.<P><BR>First.  FORGET the slander case, they are close to impossible to prove and unless it damaged your REAL BAD the courts laugh and throw them out.<P>Second.  I would make 2 calls, as this is now out of hand, take all your evidence, and calls the audit dept of the company (could you share which company it is?  No problem if you do'nt), no audit dept then call the VP Controller or Controller and tell him you have proof, people have been fired over it, and you are next.<P>Should the controller fail to do it, you have proof of theft, call the Police.  He can be charged with theft and that will force an internal audit.<P>This isn't going to be easy, it's going to be tough and difficult, stand your ground and protect yourself and those fired around you, GET IN TOUCH with them!  Tell them what is going on and get them involved.<P>Any questions reply here or email me direct.<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fieroworld.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fieroworld.com</A>  The place to go for Fiero INFO! <BR>Home of my 87 GT and 85 GT<BR>Justin C.<BR>Engineer, Designer and<BR>Technical Trainer for Nortel Networks Business Products.||||reg||1
Z||000004||TSPalmer||10-26-2001||08:48 AM||||If you do get canned, make sure you contact your local labor board too.  They usually have lawyers that eat situations like this up.  Might want to make them aware of the folks that got let go during all this.<P>------------------<BR>T.Shawn Palmer<BR>'85 SE 4cyl 5spd||||reg||3
Z||000005||falconhulk||10-26-2001||09:00 AM||||Go to a lawyer.  They have "Free consultation".  They will tell you if they think you have a case.  Do it asap.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Oreif||10-26-2001||09:05 AM||||I would gather all the info and talk with the District Attorney's office. If people are getting fired because this manager is "framing" them it is a crime. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||falconhulk||10-26-2001||09:17 AM||||Go to a lawyer first.  get their advice before goin to a DA.  They wil tell you whats in your best intrest||||reg||1
Z||000008||Ken Wittlief||10-26-2001||09:46 AM||||one important question that I dont know the answer to:<P> is this compusa store owned locally or is it owned by the coporation?<P> it makes a big difference - if its owned by compusa then the people you are dealing with are local managers who work for compusa - in which case it sounds like more than one of them are stealing from the corporation.<P> but if the store is owned locally, and they are only a franchise of compusa, then the local owner could be 'stealing from himself' taking stuff from inventory and writing it off on his insurance or taxes as lose due to thief.<P> in that case, if you complain to the compusa corporation that the local owner is stealing from his own store - they wont care - they are not losing any money and they dont care how the local owner handles his books, as long as they are getting paid for the merchandise they ship to him.<P> Personally I would cut my own losses and get the h#ll out of there. Life is too short for you to waste your time trying to clean up a corrupt computer store.<P> If you stay at best you will get a few pats on the back and you will get to keep your job - but if new managers are put in that store they will always think you are a weazle.<P> At worse these bums could pin some Kwrap on you, or if one of them really gets into legal trouble he might come looking for you some night with a 45.<P>  You made an attempt to bring this situation to the attention of the right people. If they dont want to deal with it - screw em - they dont deserve to have a great employee like you working for them.<P> Believe in yourself and your skills - go find a job where you will be happy, feel good about yourself and the people you work for - and where success will make the business a better place to work, instead of lining some slimeballs pockets.||||reg||11
Z||000009||Formula88||10-26-2001||09:47 AM||||In any event, make sure you keep copies of all your proof safe.  If the proof you send the company "disappears" you'll need a backup.  <P>Also, unless all the corrupt people at the store are fired, you're likely going to loose your job.  That, or they'll make it so miserable for you you'll want to quit.  It's not too hard for an employer to get rid of someone they don't like.  You're the lead tech.  He could simply hire a newbie and then let you go as a "downsizing" move, since I assume you make more money than the newbies.  Companies do that all the time.  By hiring someone entry level, they can let go more experienced (and expensive) employees.  <P>Definately consult with a lawyer, give all the info to HR (go as high as you can, and make sure you tell HR what's happening - retaliation is also illegal), and finally, let the Police/DA take care of the rest.  <P>And start looking for another job.  Just in case.  If things go bad, even if a lawsuit gets you re-hired - would you want to stay there?<P>Good luck!||||reg||14
Z||000010||JSocha||10-26-2001||09:51 AM||||Some of you guys are missing the obviouse.<P>First, contact the Manager via a "Certified with Signature Request" letter over your managers head and inform him that you have a problem with your current manager and that you feel he "IS" targeting you, the reasons why and possibly placing untrue documentations in your file.  Indicate which day (and before they get the letter), that you intend on requesting a complete copy of your employment file for your review.<P>Sometimes, the chain of command on the proverbial ladder to which you report needs to be broken, especially if you feel your manager is targeting you because of something else to which he has a problem with.<P>The day you send the letter to the higher up in charge of your manager as you have been doing and without your managers knowledge of the letter you sent, go in and request a complete COPY of your entire employment file and make the copies yourself right then and there.  Do Not let them do it when they want.  This is your file and you have the right to view it at any time without question for accuracy.  <P>If they say they can not do it, you are not allowed to see your file or tell you to come back later, they have already violated your rights.  There are labor laws in most states that support your right to do this at any time.<P>This way, performance evaluations do not go missing and funky stuff does not go in without your knowledge that could be untrue or unwarranted.<P>On another letter (which you also send a copy to upper management along with the other letter) only after you make a complete copy of your employement record, hand a letter of request to your manager and indicate from this day forward (the day you made the copies of your file), any item and/or documentation placed in your employement file either has to have a copy given to you and/or the original signed (copy made thereafter) that you are aware of the documentation exsistance and that you either agree or disagree to its content.  Any subsequent documentation placed in your file without your direct knowledge of its exsistance is not a valid document.  At that point, inform him about the letter you have sent to upper management (don't discuss its content and only after you get a copy of your employement file) along with the letter you just gave him requesting a copy of anything that goes into your file after it has been acknowledge by you.<P>Next, immadetly document the date and time of any behavior out of the norm and if possible, have witnesses attest to the fact.  Send a copy immedately to his manager and provide him with a copy as well while keeping the original for yourself.<P>Realize though, if your manager could be classified as golfing buddies with his manager, both can gang up on you.  So make sure there is separation between them.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||SteveJ||10-26-2001||10:26 AM||||You are getting some really, really, really bad advice in this thread.<P>The fate of whistle blowers is always bad and is never good.<P>Police, lawyers, DA's all rely on a company's internal procedures to settle these issues. Accountants hold out a slim but viable chance of working but they will have fired you long before it takes effect.<P>Make no mistake about it you were toast when you went over your manager's head about this. (He's not gone is he?) You now have to think about yourself and how you get out of this intact and with a good letter of recommendation if you can.<P>Forget about the thefts and the company problems and figure how you get out in the best possible condition. Even if you survived your manager's firing the higher-ups would never trust a whistle blower. You are perpetually labled a trouble maker in this company regardless of the truth of the issues. Regretably, it's just the way it all works.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Ken Wittlief||10-26-2001||10:29 AM||||something else you should be aware of. many small businesses keep two sets of books, one that they use to show a small profit, or even a loss, for tax purposes, and the other 'real set' that shows how much money the business is really making.<P> Its not legal - but many small busineess owners feel they could not survive if they reported all their profit and income - becasue of all the taxes they would have to pay.<P>  this in more common than you might think - and even though you think you have stumbled across 'theif from the company' you may have wandered into this aspect of your companies book keeping.<P> Its a tough call to make. If you are aware of illegal activities where you work, and you do not report them, then you have become an accomplise. <P> But on the other hand, if you have stuck your nose into a managers 'creative book keeping' which is being done to make their tax profile more 'favorable', then you might be in a grey area of business where most employers would rather that you mind your own business.<BR> <BR> Its a tough call to make. There are always things in businesses that fall between the cracks - like equipment that cant be sold for one reason or another, and the company cant use it cause it would appear as profit on the books - and sometimes the best thing to do with the stuff is throw it in the dumpster.<P> the dumpster behind the store today, or the dumpster behind the owners apartment, after he 'stores it' in his living room for 2 years.<P> please dont take this as me saying that its ok for managers to take stuff home from their buisness, or to keep two sets of books, or to cheat on their taxes. Im only pointing out that it happens a lot - so this may be another reason why compusa has not called out the national guard to raid the store - it may be a shock to you, but it might be nothing new to them.||||reg||1
Z||000013||FieroSTETZ||10-26-2001||01:22 PM||||as far as being canned, it does not really matter any more- moved back in with parents a while ago, and i can survive for a few months off of what i have saved. my only point is that if i do get fired, even if i don't, i want to become such a pain in the ass that they fire me unlawfully. my major problem is that this manager has been with the company for so long that the HR people i called (in TEXAS) were "golfing buddies" no one did anything, i have tried regional (california) and the same results. the manager knows that i am trying to do something. I am not trying to be a whistleblower, i want vengeance for the previously fired employees. It's hard not to want (i am 19, mind you) to do all sorts of vandalism, tear up his ford lightning, and the other managers BMW, but i am better than that. that would be pointless, i want to start something rolling that will inspire other employeesto stand up. conditions in this store are so bad that we are losing 9 cashiers on nov. 15th (all high school kids living with parents) they are giving no notice, just a walkout. will try hitting them up then for any additional proof. i think that about the last thing i could do is write a letter to Hal Compton, (president) and detail the working conditions, and send copies of proof of theft, etc. and let him know that i worked my way up the chain of command and was ignored the entire time. I also think that the next time i am physically threatened by our extremely violent retail manager, i will let him hit me (or otherwise, anyone seen fight club when he beats himself) the only reason i don't just up and quit is that the company is currently paying for me to study and take a bunch of cert tests i can't get on my own, CCNA, Apple, etc... I think that when i am a little healthier, I will cruise over the the labor board office and dee what they say. or, i will hire midgets to dress as oompa loompas and run throughthe store hitting people with dead trout. thanks for the advice people. any police officers reading this thread? and advice on what i can do from a legal aspect? proof of theft that we have is things returned without having been purchased, selling things to himself at far below COST, etc.. <P>is it illegal for someone to be inebriated on the job? any way for me to get someone to drug test him? I want to be an irritating bastard to the point that they cannot keep ignoring me. thanks for the responses!||||reg||1
